  "body": "https://wicg.github.io/serial/#readable-attribute says:\n\n> The [Promise](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#idl-promise) returned by this algorithm is immediately resolved so that it does not block canceling the stream. [[STREAMS](https://wicg.github.io/serial/#bib-streams)] specifies that this algorithm will not be invoked again until a chunk is enqueued.\n\nBut is this true? AFAICT https://streams.spec.whatwg.org/#readable-byte-stream-controller-should-call-pull is defined in a way that it can run for each read request. E.g. in JS:\n\n```js\nrs = new ReadableStream({\n  pull(c) {\n    console.log(\"pull called\")\n  },\n  type: \"bytes\"\n});\nreader = rs.getReader();\nreader.read();\n```\n\nAnother pull happens when you make another `reader.read()` call later, even without any enqueue.\n\nAnd I don't see the need to resolve the promise immediately, I don't see anything actually blocking the stream cancellation. See:\n\n```js\nrs = new ReadableStream({\n  pull(c) {\n    console.log(\"pull called\")\n    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {});\n  },\n  type: \"bytes\"\n});\nreader = rs.getReader();\nreadPromise = reader.read();\nreader.cancel(); // This cancels the stream immediately, even though the pull promise never resolves\n\n// or with byob reader\nreader = rs.getReader({ mode: \"byob\" });\nreadPromise = reader.read(new Uint8Array(8));\nreader.cancel(); // cancels immediately too, readPromise resolves with `{ done: true, value: undefined }`\n```\n\nCan we clarify a bit around there?",
